      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    \"please review the attached report\","
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "for msg in test_messages:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    print(f\"  '{msg}' -> {classifier.predict(msg)}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 4: Inspect the learned probabilities"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def show_top_words(classifier, cls, n=5):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    vocab_size = len(classifier.vocab)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    total = classifier.class_word_totals[cls]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    probs = {}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for word in classifier.vocab:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        count = classifier.word_counts[cls].get(word, 0)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        probs[word] = (count + classifier.smoothing) / (total + classifier.smoothing * vocab_size)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    sorted_words = sorted(probs.items(), key=lambda x: x[1], reverse=True)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for word, prob in sorted_words[:n]:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        print(f\"    {word}: {prob:.4f}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "print(\"\\nTop spam words:\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "show_top_words(classifier, \"spam\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "print(\"\\nTop ham words:\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "show_top_words(classifier, \"ham\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. **Multiple tests.** A patient tests positive twice on independent tests (both 99% accurate, disease prevalence 1 in 10,000). What is P(sick) after both tests? Use the posterior from the first test as the prior for the second.\n\n2. **Smoothing impact.** Run the spam classifier with smoothing values of 0.01, 0.1, 1.0, and 10.0. How do the top word probabilities change? What happens with smoothing=0 and a word that appears only in ham?\n\n3. **Add features.** Extend the NaiveBayes class to also use message length (short/long) as a feature alongside word counts. Estimate P(short|spam) and P(short|ham) from the training data and fold it into the prediction score.\n\n4. **MAP by hand.** Given observed data (7 heads in 10 coin flips), compute the MAP estimate of the bias using a Beta(2,2) prior. Compare it to the MLE estimate (7/10)."
      ]
    }
